  {
    "episode_id": 46445,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/6/46445.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 50,
    "turn_text": "Well, I think, first of all, you recognize that any cabinet officer, including Secretary of Defense, serves at the pleasure of the president of the United States. And you are an instrument and organ of the presidents, of his government. And not only do you serve at his pleasure, but you're expected to carry out his policies. There can be differences. There should be debate, appropriate forms for that. But I think when there gets to be a point where there is an issue between the White House and any cabinet member, where the differences become so significant that the appropriate thing is for is for that secretary to resign. And that's the way the system works, as far as Advice to a next secretary. I think that's something the next secretary makes and needs to make sure they know and understand which I'm sure they they do and will and then I think Continue to do what you think is the right thing to do for the country and Make the right choices and decisions make the right recommendations to the president not based on the president's political ideology or the president's politics, but on what you think is the right thing for the Defense Department and for our country. I've always thought the job as Secretary of Defense is really truly the one nonpartisan job in the cabinet because defense and security are not partisan issues. Defense and security is in the interest of every American, regardless of their party or their political philosophy, but you are a member of a political team. And so that position is a little different than any other position, I think. But I've always believed, as the United States Senator, when I disagreed with the Republican President on Iraq and policies. And when I was often vilified by my own party, calling me a rhino and a traitor and so on, I've said on the Senate floor, I took an oath of office to the Constitution. I didn't take an oath of office to a president or to a political party. And I think that is the guiding principle for anybody in public office. And if there comes a time when those collide, the interest in what you think, then you will have to leave. The President is not going to leave.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
